<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#poll-tax">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Poll tax</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as head tax, capitation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A poll tax, also called a head tax or capitation, is a tax of a fixed sum levied on every liable individual, usually every adult, without regard to income or resources. Such taxes were important sources of revenue from ancient times until the 19th century: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> levied a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributum">tributum</a> capitis on their provincial subjects, Islamic law imposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> on non-Muslims, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s capitation of 1695 divided French society into twenty-two classes for assessment. Because the tax is regressive, high rates provoked revolts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peasants-revolt">Peasants' Revolt</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1381, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> poll taxes were used to keep poor and African-American voters from the polls.</p></div>
